"Compares category magnitudes through bar lengths measured from a common zero baseline.\n\nUse when: Readers need accurate comparisons among discrete categories. An ordered ranking or an existing category order matters.\n\nAvoid when: Values are distributions whose shape and spread must remain visible.\n\nVisual grammar: Position identifies each category on a discrete axis. Length encodes the quantitative value in stated units.\n\nBuild: Aggregate one value per category using a named statistic. Choose horizontal bars when names need substantial reading space. Start the magnitude axis at zero and label important endpoints.\n\nCheck: Confirm all bars use identical units and aggregation. Check missing categories are distinguished from measured zero. Verify the rank follows values unless domain order takes precedence.\n\nPitfalls: A truncated baseline exaggerates length differences. Too many differently colored bars imply groups that do not exist.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic orders across six shops: sort horizontal bars by count and label each total.\n\nAlternatives: Dot plot, Grouped bar chart, Pareto chart."
